Q: How do you set up binding in MAUI using MVVM with a CheckBox, CollectionView, DataTemplate and EventToCommandBehavior? A: <p>As per docs <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/maui/fundamentals/behaviors" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/maui/fundamentals/behaviors</a> (.NET MAUI 9):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>(i) Important</p>
<p>.NET MAUI does not set the BindingContext of a behavior, because behaviors can be shared and applied to multiple controls through styles.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But this means, you can manually set the BindingContext to the behavior. Usually it's enough to set it to the same as the BindingContext of the nearest visual element, e.g.</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;CheckBox x:Name=&quot;checkBox&quot; Grid.Row=&quot;1&quot; Grid.Column=&quot;0&quot;&gt;
    &lt;CheckBox.Behaviors&gt;
        &lt;toolkit:EventToCommandBehavior
            x:DataType=&quot;models:FactorUI&quot;
            BindingContext=&quot;{Binding BindingContext, x:DataType=CheckBox, Source={Reference checkBox}&quot;
            EventName=&quot;CheckedChanged&quot;
            Command=&quot;{Binding BindingContext.BoundIntCommand, Source={x:Reference CrossingPageXML}}&quot;
                              CommandParameter=&quot;{Binding AreaId}&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/CheckBox.Behaviors&gt;
&lt;/CheckBox&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Display and edit text into TextFormField using voice (Flutter Speech to text) A: <p>Disclosure: I am the owner of the linked repo</p>
<p>You can use the package below, A Flutter widget that provides a TextField with built-in speech-to-text functionality. This widget makes it easy to add voice input capabilities to your app with minimal setup.</p>
<p>Installation</p>
<p>Add this to your package's <code>pubspec.yaml</code> file:</p>
<pre><code>dependencies:
  speech_to_text_field: ^0.1.1
</code></pre>
<p>Then run:</p>
<pre><code>flutter pub get
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://pub.dev/packages/speech_to_text_field" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://pub.dev/packages/speech_to_text_field</a></p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/KPiPFcTG.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/KPiPFcTG.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a></p>
<p>Usage</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>            SpeechToTextField(
              controller: _controller,
              labelText: 'Speak something',
              hintText: 'Tap the mic and start speaking',
              onListeningStarted: () {
                print('Started listening');
              },
              onListeningStopped: () {
                print('Stopped listening');
              },
              onListeningError: (error) {
                print('Error: $error');
              },
            ),
</code></pre>

Q: Making a javascript string sql friendly A: <p>It turns out that mysql_real_escape_string() is pretty trivial, as is its replacement <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/mysqli.real-escape-string.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>mysqli::real_escape_string</code></a>. <a href="http://php.net/manual/en/function.mysql-real-escape-string.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">According to the documentation</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>mysql_real_escape_string()</strong> calls MySQL's library function mysql_real_escape_string, which prepends backslashes to the following characters: \x00, \n, \r, , ', &quot; and \x1a.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sounds pretty simple, actually. You could do something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function mysql_real_escape_string (str) {
    return str.replace(/[\0\x08\x09\x1a\n\r&quot;'\\\%]/g, function (char) {
        switch (char) {
            case &quot;\0&quot;:
                return &quot;\\0&quot;;
            case &quot;\x08&quot;:
                return &quot;\\b&quot;;
            case &quot;\x09&quot;:
                return &quot;\\t&quot;;
            case &quot;\x1a&quot;:
                return &quot;\\z&quot;;
            case &quot;\n&quot;:
                return &quot;\\n&quot;;
            case &quot;\r&quot;:
                return &quot;\\r&quot;;
            case &quot;\&quot;&quot;:
            case &quot;'&quot;:
            case &quot;\\&quot;:
            case &quot;%&quot;:
                return &quot;\\&quot;+char; // prepends a backslash to backslash, percent,
                                  // and double/single quotes
            default:
                return char;
        }
    });
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>NOTE</strong>: I haven't run this through any sort of unit test or security test, but it does seem to work -- and, just as an added bonus, it escapes tabs, backspaces, and '%' so it can also be used in LIKE queries, as per <a href="https://www.owasp.org/index.php/SQL_Injection_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet#MySQL_Escaping" rel="nofollow noreferrer">OWASP's recommendations</a> (unlike the PHP original).</p>
<p>I do know that <code>mysql_real_escape_string()</code> is character-set-aware, but I'm not sure what benefit that adds.</p>
<p>There's a good discussion of these issues <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/229889/sanitizing-mysql-user-parameters">over here</a>.</p>
